coreaudio: a block without a date starts the output at once

Seeked into the last second of a file, the input can reach its end before
the audio output has played anything since the seek (every block late or
none decoded yet). At the end the decoder drains: the resampler (soxr)
hands over what it holds in a block without a date, and that block, the
first one since the seek, set the output's first render time from a date
of 0 less the latency: far beyond any time the render callback reaches.
The render callback then put out silence for ever, the drain
(ca_Flush with wait) waited for the buffer to empty for ever, and the
input never reported its end ("waiting decoder fifos to empty"). Such a
block starts the output at once. Applied after 0004, with patch -p1
(Scripts/build_vlckit_lgpl.sh).

diff --git a/modules/audio_output/coreaudio_common.c b/modules/audio_output/coreaudio_common.c
--- a/modules/audio_output/coreaudio_common.c
+++ b/modules/audio_output/coreaudio_common.c
@@ -355,7 +355,13 @@
          * first (non-silence/zero) frame is rendered by the render callback.
          * Once the rendering is truly started, the date can be ignored. */
 
-        const vlc_tick_t first_render_time = p_block->i_pts - ca_GetLatencyLocked(p_aout);
+        /* Changed for Schnellbild on 2026-11-16: Scripts/vlc-patches/0005-coreaudio-a-block-without-a-date-starts-the-output-at-once.patch */
+        /* A block without a date (what a resampler hands over when it is
+         * drained at the end, with nothing played since a seek) starts at
+         * once: its date taken as 0 put the start far beyond any time the
+         * render callback reaches, and draining waited for it for ever */
+        const vlc_tick_t first_render_time = p_block->i_pts != VLC_TICK_INVALID
+            ? p_block->i_pts - ca_GetLatencyLocked(p_aout) : mdate();
         p_sys->i_first_render_host_time =
             TickToHostTime(p_sys, first_render_time);
     }
